  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nSurface low pressure will deepen over northwest Kansas today with \nincreasing south wind speeds and very warm temperatures over \ncentral/eastern Kansas. Daytime highs will be well above normal \nagain today and Saturday with heat indices around 100 degrees\nover southern Kansas. Current satellite water vapor imagery \nshows a healthy upper level trough over the northwestern states,\nand a lead short wave over the southwestern states. The lead \nshort wave will move northeast and reach eastern Colorado by \ntonight. This lead wave will spark off showers/storms in eastern\nColorado eastward across northwest Kansas into eastern Nebraska\ntonight. Meanwhile the parent upper trough will continue to move\neast across the Rockies and eject out into Nebraska and the \nDakotas by Sunday night. A frontal boundary will sag slowly \nsouthward across Kansas for Saturday into Saturday night with \nscattered to numerous showers/storms developing along the front \nespecially at night. A few strong to severe storms are possible \nwith modest instability and wind shear around 30kts expected. \nThere is also some potential for locally heavy rainfall Saturday\nnight. Cooler air will finally begin to push southward across \nthe region for Sunday along with lingering shower/storm chances.\n\nHeading into next week noticeably cooler air will finally overspread \nthe entire region in the wake of the upper level wave moving \neastward across the northern plains. Dry weather is also expected \nwith drier air in place as surface high pressure dominates over the \ncentral plains. Long range models are hinting at the next chance for \nshowers/storms possibly arriving late Wednesday night into\nThursday.",
